WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.240
Speaker 1: We're starting today with the&nbsp;
topic of abortion. And this is a topic that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:08.480
there was a period of time where it was less&nbsp;
of an issue. There was less of a question as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:13.840
to whether Roe v Wade was going to remain the&nbsp;
law of the land. But now, with the Supreme Court&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:20.160
potentially just weeks away from issuing a ruling&nbsp;
that would uphold state restrictions on abortion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:26.400
which would effectively reverse Roe v&nbsp;
Wade, and the undue burden standard that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:32.560
Roe v Wade contains, which we will get to&nbsp;
that we are seen expectedly as predicted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:38.800
more and more states passed the types of laws that&nbsp;
we have seen from Mississippi and from Texas and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:45.520
from elsewhere. And the latest state to do this&nbsp;
is now Oklahoma. Oklahoma lawmakers have passed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:53.120
a six week abortion, banned a ban modeled after&nbsp;
the Texas law that allows civil enforcement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:59.520
That's another element of this that is wacky,&nbsp;
retrograde stuff. Civil enforcement of this law.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:04.880
And we will talk about why a six week&nbsp;
abortion ban is effectively just a ban&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:10.240
on abortion, which unquestionably would&nbsp;
violate Roe v Wade undue burden clause,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:16.320
except Roe v Wade may be reversed. So let's read&nbsp;
about this first Associated Press reporting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:22.320
Oklahoma lawmakers Thursday gave final approval&nbsp;
to a bill modeled after the controversial Texas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:27.520
abortion law. We knew every state that wants to&nbsp;
ban abortion is going to start using that type of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:36.800
the they will use each other as models. It is a&nbsp;
law which allows private citizens to take civil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:43.520
action against abortion providers as individuals&nbsp;
to enforce the law. It's a sort of vigilantism in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:52.000
practice. The Oklahoma Heartbeat Bill, Heartbeat&nbsp;
Act Senate Bill 1503 would prohibit abortions at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:58.560
the time when a physician can detect early cardiac&nbsp;
activity in an embryo or fetus. Now understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:05.600
that it is not. It had some like to say&nbsp;
it is a heartbeat. And doctors often say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:10.800
it's this is reflexive cardiac activity.&nbsp;
To call it a heartbeat isn't even accurate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:17.360
That can be as early as six weeks into pregnancy&nbsp;
before many women even know they are pregnant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:24.000
The measure also provides exceptions for medical&nbsp;
emergencies. The state Senate later Thursday also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:32.400
passed House Bill 4327, allowing private citizens&nbsp;
to sue abortion providers. House Bill 4327,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:37.680
however, would prohibit abortions at any point of&nbsp;
pregnancy with exceptions for medical emergencies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:44.000
or if the pregnancy comes as the result of rape&nbsp;
or sexual assault, incest and is reported to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:49.200
law enforcement. So there's two parts of this.&nbsp;
First, we've already talked because of the Texas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:54.560
bill about this ability for an individual to&nbsp;
sue the abortion provider. It's another way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:59.440
to put in this case, it's not only the&nbsp;
government between doctors and their patients,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:05.120
but random people between doctors and their&nbsp;
patients, if anybody can bring a civil suit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:12.320
But I want to focus in on the six week ban thing,&nbsp;
because the defenders of this bill who want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:18.560
at least pretend about the rule of law, which is&nbsp;
Roe v Wade, established that generally speaking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:26.000
women should have access to abortion services and&nbsp;
that no undue burden should be placed on them in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:32.000
order to obtain one. Now, undue burden is a legal&nbsp;
term that subject to interpretation, and this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:38.080
where I've been for 40 years, that that phrase&nbsp;
undue burden has been subject to interpretation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:44.320
by courts at all levels. But a six week ban would&nbsp;
absolutely violate that and just think it through.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:50.240
Many women most women don't know that&nbsp;
they're pregnant until 4 to 5 weeks. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:56.880
what I mean by that is, by the time you miss&nbsp;
a period and by the time most home pregnancy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:04.240
tests can detect a pregnancy, you're already&nbsp;
considered to be four or five weeks pregnant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:10.160
at minimum. If you at that point were to&nbsp;
say, hey, I got a positive pregnancy test,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:17.360
I'm going to go to my doctor, my OB-GYN. They&nbsp;
will say, Oh, yeah, it's not your pregnant. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:22.640
it's the first week they would say you are&nbsp;
practically speaking for potentially five weeks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:28.160
pregnant at this point of time. And if that if&nbsp;
the requirement is no abortions after six weeks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:34.080
what that means is you need to say, oh, well, I&nbsp;
need an abortion like tomorrow. And of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:40.400
that is unreasonable. That is an undue burden,&nbsp;
even if one can decide that quickly, too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:44.240
If most of you have tried to get medical&nbsp;
appointments lately, you might know you don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:48.160
call and they don't give you an appointment for&nbsp;
tomorrow. That's generally a difficult thing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:54.320
do. So in practical terms, this is designed to be&nbsp;
a total abortion ban and it effectively would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.880 --> 00:05:03.040
by enforcing the six week rule. We will see.&nbsp;
What happens in the Supreme Court. This bill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

